NAME
       ss - show sockets and the ports they use

SYNOPSIS
       **ss** [**-tulanpesH4**]

DESCRIPTION
       Lists TCP and UDP sockets: state, queued bytes, local and peer
       address and the process that owns the socket. Ports handled inside
       the kernel (for example the kernel DHCP client on UDP 68) are marked
       "kernel". Without -a or -l only connected sockets are shown.

       Service names come from /etc/services. An address bound to one
       interface is written as ADDRESS%DEVICE:PORT.

       Other users' processes are hidden unless run as root.

OPTIONS
       **-t**, **--tcp**          TCP sockets
       **-u**, **--udp**          UDP sockets
       **-l**, **--listening**    only listening sockets (TCP LISTEN, UDP UNCONN)
       **-a**, **--all**          listening and connected sockets
       **-n**, **--numeric**      print port numbers, not service names
       **-p**, **--processes**    show users:(("name",pid=N,fd=N))
       **-e**, **--extended**     show uid and reuseaddr/broadcast
       **-s**, **--summary**      print counters only
       **-H**, **--no-header**    no header line

EXAMPLES
       ss -tulnp        everything that listens, with processes
       sudo ss -ap      all sockets of all users
       ss -s            totals

SEE ALSO
       ip(8), netd(8), host(1)
